WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.420
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.909
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:16.900
Unit fifty-four: Science and Belief. In this unit, you will distinguish science from belief and evaluate evidence.

4
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.442
scientific literacy.

5
00:00:20.142 --> 00:00:27.238
scientific literacy means the ability to understand and evaluate scientific information.

6
00:00:27.538 --> 00:00:32.138
Scientific literacy helps citizens judge health claims.

7
00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:36.547
Your turn. Say: scientific literacy.

8
00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:40.910
scientific literacy.

9
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:43.185
pseudoscience.

10
00:00:43.885 --> 00:00:49.216
pseudoscience means beliefs presented as science but without scientific evidence.

11
00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:52.828
Astrology is a classic example of pseudoscience.

12
00:00:53.528 --> 00:00:56.610
Your turn. Say: pseudoscience.

13
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.624
pseudoscience.

14
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:02.722
scepticism.

15
00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:08.011
scepticism means an attitude of doubting claims until there is evidence.

16
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.088
Healthy scepticism protects us from scams.

17
00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:15.102
Your turn. Say: scepticism.

18
00:01:17.802 --> 00:01:18.943
scepticism.

19
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.369
falsifiability.

20
00:01:22.069 --> 00:01:28.411
falsifiability means the possibility of proving a theory wrong through testing.

21
00:01:28.711 --> 00:01:32.231
Falsifiability separates science from pseudoscience.

22
00:01:32.931 --> 00:01:36.640
Your turn. Say: falsifiability.

23
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.747
falsifiability.

24
00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.030
burden of proof.

25
00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:47.157
burden of proof means the duty to provide evidence for a claim.

26
00:01:47.457 --> 00:01:50.571
The burden of proof lies with the person making the claim.

27
00:01:51.271 --> 00:01:54.330
Your turn. Say: burden of proof.

28
00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.182
burden of proof.

29
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.823
confirmation bias.

30
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:07.110
confirmation bias means noticing only information that supports what we already believe.

31
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.965
Confirmation bias makes us ignore inconvenient facts.

32
00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:14.979
Your turn. Say: confirmation bias.

33
00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:19.319
confirmation bias.

34
00:02:20.317 --> 00:02:21.968
anecdotal evidence.

35
00:02:22.668 --> 00:02:28.150
anecdotal evidence means evidence based on personal stories rather than research.

36
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.518
Anecdotal evidence is not enough to prove a cure.

37
00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:35.486
Your turn. Say: anecdotal evidence.

38
00:02:38.186 --> 00:02:39.698
anecdotal evidence.

39
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:41.893
superstition.

40
00:02:42.593 --> 00:02:47.089
superstition means a belief in luck or magic not based on reason.

41
00:02:47.389 --> 00:02:50.515
Many superstitions are part of cultural tradition.

42
00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:54.262
Your turn. Say: superstition.

43
00:02:56.962 --> 00:02:58.009
superstition.

44
00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:01.272
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:01.572 --> 00:03:04.848
Ms. Sarah, my aunt swears that a herbal drink cured her cold.

46
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.914
That's anecdotal evidence, on which many health claims rely.

47
00:03:11.217 --> 00:03:13.019
But she really did get better.

48
00:03:14.317 --> 00:03:19.695
Most colds disappear within a week anyway, which is something people often forget.

49
00:03:20.983 --> 00:03:23.598
So how do scientists decide what works?

50
00:03:24.883 --> 00:03:29.463
Through tests in which the claim could be proved wrong. That's falsifiability.

51
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.388
And if someone makes an extraordinary claim?

52
00:03:34.683 --> 00:03:40.166
The burden of proof lies with them, regardless of whom they know or how confident they sound.

53
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.133
Grammar focus: Advanced relative clauses (mastery).

54
00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:48.327
Relative clauses can refer to a whole idea.

55
00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:52.112
Colds disappear anyway, which is something people forget.

56
00:03:52.412 --> 00:03:55.851
Tests in which the claim could be proved wrong are essential.

57
00:03:56.151 --> 00:04:00.960
A sentential relative clause (', which ...') comments on a whole previous idea.

58
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.194
Now read and listen. How Do We Know What Is True?.

59
00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:10.025
Every day, we hear claims about health, history and the natural world.

60
00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:16.079
Science offers a method for testing these claims, which is based on evidence and doubt.

61
00:04:17.383 --> 00:04:22.796
According to philosopher Karl Popper, a scientific theory must be falsifiable.

62
00:04:24.083 --> 00:04:30.355
Pseudoscience, by contrast, makes claims that cannot be tested or that ignore contrary evidence.

63
00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.390
Confirmation bias leads us to remember successes and forget failures.

64
00:04:37.683 --> 00:04:43.352
Anecdotal evidence, however convincing it feels, cannot replace controlled studies.

65
00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:49.831
Healthy scepticism does not mean rejecting everything, but asking for evidence.

66
00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:58.468
Scientific literacy, in which these skills are combined, may be one of the most important skills of our time.

67
00:04:59.783 --> 00:05:02.424
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:02.724 --> 00:05:06.221
Beliefs presented as science without evidence are blank.

69
00:05:06.521 --> 00:05:12.915
A, pseudoscience. B, scepticism. C, falsifiability.

70
00:05:16.917 --> 00:05:19.172
The answer is A: pseudoscience.

71
00:05:20.683 --> 00:05:22.787
He missed the bus, blank made him late.

72
00:05:23.087 --> 00:05:26.416
A, what. B, which. C, that.

73
00:05:30.417 --> 00:05:32.718
The answer is B: which.

74
00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:36.797
It's a theory blank many doctors disagree.

75
00:05:37.097 --> 00:05:40.715
A, with which. B, which. C, whom.

76
00:05:44.717 --> 00:05:47.320
The answer is A: with which.

77
00:05:48.817 --> 00:05:51.803
Evidence based on personal stories is blank.

78
00:05:52.103 --> 00:05:58.415
A, anecdotal evidence. B, burden of proof. C, scientific literacy.

79
00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:05.542
The answer is A: anecdotal evidence.

80
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:11.290
According to Popper, a scientific theory must be blank.

81
00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:15.766
A, popular. B, falsifiable. C, ancient.

82
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.783
The answer is B: falsifiable.

83
00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:30.999
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can people protect themselves from false health claims?

84
00:06:36.299 --> 00:06:42.594
Part two: Use a sentential relative clause, give two strategies and one example.

85
00:06:48.894 --> 00:07:02.335
People should ask for controlled evidence, which most false claims cannot provide. They should also check whether trusted health organisations agree. My uncle almost bought a 'miracle cure', which turned out to be completely untested.

86
00:07:02.635 --> 00:07:04.797
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:05.783 --> 00:07:11.756
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced relative clauses (mastery).

88
00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:16.891
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:17.191 --> 00:07:20.238
See you in Unit fifty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:20.538 --> 00:07:23.179
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

